1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,300
Hello, welcome to NALI 2026,

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:07,340
the New Academia Learning
Innovation event

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,020
organised by UTM CDEx.

4
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:14,080
Held on October 28–29 at
Universiti Teknologi Malaysia,

5
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,380
it brought together educators and

6
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:19,600
innovators under the theme
“Human–AI Synergy in Pedagogy.”

7
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:24,210
With keynotes, workshops, an innovation
competition and teaching awards,

8
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:28,500
NALI connects teaching and research
for the future of education.

9
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:32,650
In this video, we interviewed six
talented inventors about their creations

10
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:34,900
and how they can enhance
student learning.

11
00:00:35,713 --> 00:00:37,383
Hello. I’m Wong Wen Hao.

12
00:00:37,413 --> 00:00:41,818
During our NALI 2026 visit, I explored
the AI Japanese Learning Assistant.

13
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:45,168
A project that uses AI to support
Japanese language learning.

14
00:00:45,183 --> 00:00:48,818
In a large class, students
have different abilities,

15
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:51,518
and teachers have limited time
to give individual feedback.

16
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:56,018
This project combines pronunciation
practice, vocabulary, grammar correction,

17
00:00:56,033 --> 00:00:58,768
and guided conversation to support
learning beyond the classroom.

18
00:00:58,783 --> 00:01:02,018
I spoke with the project team
to understand how it works,

19
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:04,638
the challenges they face, and
their plans for improvement.

20
00:01:04,653 --> 00:01:05,438
Here is our interview.

21
00:01:05,453 --> 00:01:07,438
Could you briefly introduce your project?

22
00:01:07,453 --> 00:01:13,538
Our project is the AI Japanese
Language Learning Assistant.

23
00:01:13,553 --> 00:01:19,438
It is for students who
are learning Japanese,

24
00:01:19,453 --> 00:01:25,438
helping them improve their
pronunciation in the first stage.

25
00:01:25,453 --> 00:01:28,438
The second stage is vocabulary.

26
00:01:28,453 --> 00:01:32,438
The third stage is grammar correction.

27
00:01:32,453 --> 00:01:38,518
Finally, students practise
daily conversations.

28
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:44,478
What is the main objective for
students learning Japanese?

29
00:01:44,493 --> 00:01:48,478
To explain the objective,

30
00:01:48,493 --> 00:01:53,518
we need to return to the problems we face.

31
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:59,518
When we teach university classes, we
find that the classes are too big.

32
00:01:59,533 --> 00:02:02,518
There is limited teaching time.

33
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:08,518
Another issue is that students have

34
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:11,518
different learning abilities and levels.

35
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:19,518
How can we help them handle
the Japanese language?

36
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:26,518
We hope students will
not only attend classes,

37
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:31,518
but continue practising after class.

38
00:02:31,533 --> 00:02:38,518
My team member, Dr Yang, will show
how the assistant helps students

39
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:43,518
keep track of their studies,

40
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:46,518
while reducing lecturers’ workload,

41
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:51,558
so lecturers can understand
the students’ level.

42
00:02:51,573 --> 00:02:55,558
What are the future plans
to improve the project?

43
00:02:55,573 --> 00:02:59,558
We are currently in the first stage.

44
00:02:59,573 --> 00:03:04,558
We hope to obtain more grants

45
00:03:04,573 --> 00:03:12,558
to develop these apps
through the remaining stages.

46
00:03:12,573 --> 00:03:18,558
The app is uploaded to Google
Play, but it is in alpha testing.

47
00:03:18,573 --> 00:03:23,558
We need more testers’ feedback
before it can be published.

48
00:03:23,573 --> 00:03:31,558
In the future, we will record
students’ learning progress.

49
00:03:31,573 --> 00:03:38,558
Lecturers can then use that progress

50
00:03:38,573 --> 00:03:41,558
to customise and improve the content.

51
00:03:41,573 --> 00:03:48,558
How does your innovation balance
AI with the need for human empathy?

52
00:03:48,573 --> 00:03:53,558
This is a language-learning app.

53
00:03:53,573 --> 00:04:00,558
When we talk about empathy, I
would say it is more suitable

54
00:04:00,573 --> 00:04:06,558
for supporting students’
adaptive learning.

55
00:04:06,573 --> 00:04:13,558
Lastly, as an educational tool,

56
00:04:13,573 --> 00:04:16,558
you need to handle sensitive student data.

57
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:23,558
How do you handle security
and students’ data privacy?

58
00:04:23,573 --> 00:04:28,758
If you want to handle
sensitive data privacy,

59
00:04:28,773 --> 00:04:32,758
the application should use

60
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:39,758
a local AI token.

61
00:04:39,773 --> 00:04:45,758
It cannot use DeepSeek anymore.

62
00:04:45,773 --> 00:04:50,758
You would need to create
your own AI server.

63
00:04:50,773 --> 00:04:56,758
There are AI token costs to consider.

64
00:04:56,773 --> 00:05:00,888
There is a cost involved.

65
00:05:00,903 --> 00:05:04,208
This interview changed how
I evaluate educational AI.

66
00:05:04,223 --> 00:05:07,008
A useful system must do
more than produce an answer.

67
00:05:07,023 --> 00:05:10,408
Its feedback must help students
understand and correct a mistake.

68
00:05:10,423 --> 00:05:13,208
The pronunciation demonstration
made this concrete.

69
00:05:13,223 --> 00:05:16,043
Immediate feedback could support
independent practice, but an

70
00:05:16,063 --> 00:05:19,313
incorrect assessment could also
reinforce the wrong pronunciation.

71
00:05:19,573 --> 00:05:21,768
Teachers therefore remain
important for checking feedback and

72
00:05:21,783 --> 00:05:22,808
guiding learners.

73
00:05:22,823 --> 00:05:26,018
This connects to technology and
information systems because the

74
00:05:26,033 --> 00:05:29,548
experience depends on user input,
network access, AI processing

75
00:05:29,563 --> 00:05:31,488
and feedback working together.

76
00:05:31,503 --> 00:05:34,428
The interview also revealed a
distinction between personalisation

77
00:05:34,443 --> 00:05:35,368
and empathy.

78
00:05:35,383 --> 00:05:38,393
Adapting practice to a student's
level does not necessarily mean

79
00:05:38,663 --> 00:05:40,833
understanding their
frustration or motivation.

80
00:05:41,093 --> 00:05:43,833
Internet dependence and token
costs further showed that a working

81
00:05:44,343 --> 00:05:47,288
prototype is not the same as a
sustainable classroom system.

82
00:05:47,303 --> 00:05:50,208
The team discussed local AI
infrastructure as a possible

83
00:05:50,223 --> 00:05:53,648
privacy-related alternative
rather than an existing feature.

84
00:05:53,663 --> 00:05:57,103
For my future projects, I would
evaluate feedback accuracy, data

85
00:05:57,143 --> 00:05:59,873
handling, running costs and
teacher involvement alongside

86
00:06:00,383 --> 00:06:03,388
functionality. The visit helped
me connect course concepts with

87
00:06:03,403 --> 00:06:06,358
real design trade-offs and made
me more interested in building

88
00:06:06,373 --> 00:06:09,063
systems that support both
learners and teachers.

89
00:06:09,201 --> 00:06:12,641
That concludes my reflection on the
AI Japanese Learning Assistant.

